@Arcanist Lupus: I feel like JMS probably would have done that if he could pull it off. :P I mean, look at the ending of the episode &quot;And the Rock Cried Out, No Hiding Place&quot;.

But now that I think about it, I think Babylon 5 went a long way towards solidifying my love of blending silliness with dead seriousness (hard to name a character in that show that wasn&#39;t eccentric in some way), and of interesting ethnical conundrums.

I mean let&#39;s face it, that scene with G&#39;Kar commenting about &quot;<span style="color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: &quot;Open Sans&quot;, HelveticaNeue, &quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14.4px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">We were the good guys; they were the bad guys. &quot; is arguably the only time in the entire series you could say that with a straight face.&nbsp; And that entire episode is about someone going mad and thinking they&#39;re King Arthur because (spoilers for B5 neophytes) [color=white]they can&#39;t cope with being the person who fired the shot that accidentally almost got his species wiped out. And in the end gets absolution from the &quot;Lady of the Lake&quot; in the form of one of the leaders of the former &quot;enemy&quot; side (who struggles with her own regretful decision in that war) choosing to forgive him.[/color] I mean, dang.
